{
  "correction_rules": [
    "Horizon-dependent bias: forecasts are close for days 1–10, but from ~day 12 onward they become systematically too high (positive bias grows with horizon). Apply an increasing downward correction with lead time (small at t+1, moderate by t+10, large by t+20–30).",
    "Over-optimistic rebound/mean reversion: after a downtrend, the model predicts a stronger and faster recovery than realized. Damp any upward slope in the forecast when the last 2–4 weeks show negative momentum (reduce forecast drift toward higher prices).",
    "Overshoot after local peaks: when the model projects a mid-horizon rise into the mid-70s, realized prices continue drifting lower/sideways. Cap upside extensions: if forecast implies >~+3 to +5 EUR above the last observed price within 10–20 days during a bearish regime, scale that move down materially.",
    "Regime filter: if the 30d history shows a clear declining channel (lower highs/lows), treat the model path as too 'range-bound/high'. Re-anchor the forecast to a lower baseline (e.g., subtract a constant offset) and increase the strength of mean reversion downward rather than upward.",
    "Late-horizon variance too high: the model’s day 20–30 swings are larger than realized. Apply smoothing/shrinkage to the day 15–30 path (pull toward a gently declining/flat trajectory rather than pronounced oscillations)."
  ]
}
